// Cards per lesson. fp = first-principle card. Concept: t, b (HTML). Likely question: q, a.
// Pattern: root problem -> idea that follows -> evidence from our numbers.
const LESSONS = [
  {
    id: "00", title: "Architecture",
    cards: [
      { fp: true, t: "Fair numbers need shared parts", b: "The lab compares many ways to answer questions over docs. A score means something only when everything else stays the same.<br><b>So:</b> one shared library (<code>lab/</code>), one pinned doc set, one golden set. Each lesson is one short script that changes one thing." },
      { t: "Why thin lesson scripts?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> if each lesson had its own chunker or prompt, a score change could come from anywhere.<br><b>So:</b> lessons import the same parts and add only the new idea.<br><b>Evidence:</b> lesson 1 is 26 lines; lesson 2 adds two small functions. <code>lessons/01_naive_rag/run.py:7-13</code> <code>lessons/02_hybrid_rerank/run.py:12-17</code>" },
      { t: "Where state lives", b: "<b>Disk, <code>data/</code> (not in git):</b> pinned docs <code>scripts/fetch_docs.py:5-6</code>, vector cache <code>lab/index.py:12-19</code>, agent sessions <code>lab/agent.py:47-51</code>, run logs <code>lab/durable.py:61-65</code>.<br><b>Git, <code>evals/</code>:</b> golden set, judge labels, baseline.<br><b>Memory only:</b> the BM25 index, built again each run <code>lab/retrieve.py:13-16</code>." },
      { t: "Why one search() shape?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> many retrievers must be measured the same way.<br><b>So:</b> each one is <code>search(query, k)</code> and returns (chunk, score) pairs. One <code>evaluate</code> takes any of them.<br><b>Cost:</b> a retriever that needs more input (filters) does not fit. <code>lab/evals.py:33-38</code> <code>lessons/02_hybrid_rerank/run.py:21-24</code>" },
      { t: "Why numpy, not a vector database?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> the corpus is small. A database adds setup and hides the math.<br><b>So:</b> one matrix and a dot product, cached on disk by model + content hash.<br><b>Cost:</b> no ANN, and all vectors sit in RAM. Too slow past ~1M chunks. <code>lab/index.py:9-24</code>" },
      { t: "Why hide the LLM behind three functions?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> models and providers change. Lesson code should not.<br><b>So:</b> <code>complete</code>, <code>chat</code>, <code>chat_json</code> over any OpenAI-compatible API, picked by env vars.<br><b>Cost:</b> provider details still leak, like DeepSeek's cache field. <code>lab/llm.py:11-34</code> <code>lab/agent.py:102</code>" },
      { t: "Why no agent framework?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a framework hides the loop, and the loop is what you must understand.<br><b>So:</b> a hand-written loop first, then one class that adds checkpoint, hooks, compaction, budget.<br><b>Cost:</b> you own every edge case. <code>lessons/06_agent_loop/run.py:18-32</code> <code>lab/agent.py:90-118</code>" },
    ],
    qa: [
      { q: "Walk me through the architecture.", a: "Root: fair comparison needs shared parts. Offline: fetch pinned docs, split by heading, embed, cache the vectors. Per question: a lesson script calls the retrievers (dense, BM25, RRF, rerank), then LLM prompts (answer, grade, verify), or runs the agent loop with tools and hooks. Around it: golden set, judge, regression gate. Trade-off: one process, no service. <code>lab/chunk.py:58-60</code> <code>lab/tools.py:10-15</code>" },
      { q: "Why is it split into lab/ and lessons/?", a: "<code>lab/</code> holds what stays the same between methods. <code>lessons/</code> hold what changes. So the diff between two lessons is the idea itself. Trade-off: a lesson's prompts live in that lesson, so CRAG's grader can't be reused elsewhere without moving it. <code>lessons/04_crag/run.py:15-28</code>" },
      { q: "What happens if you delete data/?", a: "Nothing is lost for good, because data/ only holds things code can rebuild. Docs download again from the pinned commit. Vectors are embedded again (slow first run). Agent sessions are gone, so a crashed run can't resume. The hand-made state is in evals/, in git. <code>scripts/fetch_docs.py:19-25</code> <code>lab/index.py:14-19</code>" },
      { q: "Where would you add a new retriever, for example a vector database?", a: "The rest of the code only needs <code>search(query, k)</code> returning (chunk, score). So add a class with that method in lab/. <code>evaluate</code> and the lessons work as before. One trade-off: <code>Hybrid</code> builds <code>VectorIndex</code> itself, so it needs a small change too. <code>lab/evals.py:33-38</code> <code>lab/retrieve.py:53-55</code>" },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "01", title: "Naive RAG",
    job: "Chunk, embed, search once, answer with citations.", entry: "lessons/01_naive_rag/run.py:13", uses: [],
    cards: [
      { fp: true, t: "Why RAG exists", b: "An LLM only knows its training data, and it can read only a limited amount of text per call. It can't read all the docs for every question.<br><b>So:</b> pick the few pieces that matter, give only those, and make the model answer from them. That is RAG. Every other part follows from this." },
      { t: "Why chunk?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> you retrieve and pay for whole units of text. A whole doc is too big and has too much noise.<br><b>So:</b> split it into chunks. The chunk is the unit you match and the unit the LLM reads.<br><b>Trade-off:</b> small = precise but loses context; big = keeps context but adds noise and tokens. <code>lab/chunk.py:23-55</code>" },
      { t: "Why embeddings?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> users and docs use different words for the same idea.<br><b>So:</b> map text to a vector where similar meaning = nearby. Then \"find relevant text\" becomes \"find nearby vectors\". <code>lab/embed.py:23-31</code>" },
      { t: "Why normalize vectors?", b: "Cosine = a·b / (|a| |b|). If every vector has length 1, cosine is just the dot product.<br><b>So:</b> search over all chunks is one matrix multiply: <code>vecs @ q</code>. Brute force is fine up to ~1M chunks; after that, an ANN index (HNSW) trades a little accuracy for speed. <code>lab/index.py:21-24</code>" },
      { t: "Why prepend the heading?", b: "A chunk is cut from its context. A chunk that only says \"UTC\" matches nothing.<br><b>So:</b> each chunk carries <code>[doc] Heading &gt; Sub-heading</code>. Now the chunk says what it is about. <code>lab/chunk.py:16-20</code>" },
      { t: "Why a grounding prompt?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> when the chunks don't have the answer, the model fills the gap from memory. It sounds just as sure.<br><b>So:</b> \"answer only from sources, cite [n], else say 'Not in the sources'\". The cheapest guard against hallucination. <code>lab/rag.py:4-5</code>" },
      { t: "Where naive RAG breaks", b: "One question -> one vector -> one point in meaning space. If the answer needs two facts from two docs, the question vector sits <i>between</i> them and matches neither well.<br><b>Evidence:</b> single-hop hit@5 0.96, multi-hop <b>0.40</b>. <code>lessons/01_naive_rag/run.py:16-19</code>" },
      { t: "How to measure retrieval", b: "<b>hit@k</b>: is the right chunk in the top k? That tells you if a right answer is <i>possible</i>.<br><b>MRR</b>: average of 1/rank. It matters when the LLM sees only the top few.<br>A hit = right doc <b>and</b> a key phrase. Chunk IDs change with chunking; phrases don't, so strategies stay comparable. <code>lab/evals.py:19-21</code> <code>lab/evals.py:33-48</code>" },
    ],
    qa: [
      { q: "Why do we need RAG at all? Why not put all docs in the prompt?", a: "Context is limited and costs tokens every call. Quality also drops as context grows. And the model's training data is old or missing your private docs. So retrieve the few relevant pieces, and make the answer come from them. <code>lab/rag.py:12-15</code>" },
      { q: "How do you choose chunk size?", a: "The chunk is both the search unit and the reading unit. Too small: the match is precise but the meaning is cut. Too big: noise and cost. Start from the doc structure (headings), add the heading path to each chunk, then tune size on the eval set. <code>lab/chunk.py:31-41</code>" },
      { q: "What are the main ways naive RAG fails, and what is the root cause of each?", a: "1) Wording or exact-ID mismatch: the embedding lost the exact token -> hybrid. 2) Right chunk ranked too low: the fast similarity is rough -> reranker. 3) Multi-fact questions: one vector can't be close to two facts -> decomposition / multi-hop. 4) The model goes beyond the context: it fills gaps from memory -> grounding prompt, citations, groundedness check. <code>lab/retrieve.py:43-58</code>" },
      { q: "hit@k vs MRR - which matters when?", a: "hit@k asks whether the answer is possible at all. MRR asks how high the right chunk is. If you give the LLM only the top 3, rank matters, so watch MRR. <code>lab/evals.py:39-43</code>" },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "02", title: "Hybrid search + rerank",
    job: "Find more: keyword search, rank fusion, reranker.", entry: "lab/retrieve.py:50", uses: ["01"],
    cards: [
      { fp: true, t: "Two kinds of matching", b: "An embedding compresses text into a few hundred numbers. Compression keeps meaning but loses rare exact tokens like <code>-1021</code>. A keyword index keeps exact tokens but knows no meaning.<br><b>So:</b> they fail on <i>different</i> questions. Use both." },
      { t: "Evidence: different misses", b: "Dense hit@5 0.96 (missed \"trailingDelta of 100\"). BM25 0.96 (missed \"default recvWindow\"). Hybrid: <b>1.00</b>.<br>Same score, different misses. That is the whole case for hybrid. <code>lessons/02_hybrid_rerank/run.py:20-24</code>" },
      { t: "Why fuse by rank (RRF)?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> BM25 scores (0-30) and cosine (0-1) are not in the same unit, and their spread changes per query.<br><b>So:</b> use only rank: <code>score = sum 1/(60 + rank)</code>. No units, no tuning. <code>lab/retrieve.py:24-33</code>" },
      { t: "Speed vs precision", b: "To be fast, chunk vectors must be computed <i>before</i> the question exists. So question and chunk never \"see\" each other (<b>bi-encoder</b>).<br>A <b>cross-encoder</b> reads both together: more precise, but it can't pre-compute. Cost grows with every pair. <code>lab/retrieve.py:43-47</code>" },
      { t: "So: two stages", b: "Cheap and wide first (bi-encoder + BM25, top 20), expensive and narrow second (cross-encoder, keep 5).<br><i>Bi-encoder to recall, cross-encoder to rank.</i><br>A reranker only reorders. It can't find what stage 1 missed. <code>lessons/02_hybrid_rerank/run.py:12-17</code>" },
      { t: "Averages hide flips", b: "A bigger reranker raised MRR to 0.85 but <b>lost one question</b>. On 25 questions, one question = 0.04.<br><b>So:</b> read the misses, not only the average. Don't claim a win from one flip. <code>lab/retrieve.py:36-40</code>" },
    ],
    qa: [
      { q: "Why hybrid search? Explain from how embeddings work.", a: "An embedding is a lossy compression of meaning. Rare exact strings (error codes, endpoints, tickers) get blurred. BM25 keeps exact tokens but misses synonyms. They fail on different questions, so fusing them covers both. At an exchange, exact IDs are everywhere. <code>lab/retrieve.py:9-10</code> <code>lab/retrieve.py:50-58</code>" },
      { q: "Why RRF and not a weighted sum of scores?", a: "The scores are in different units and their spread shifts per query, so a fixed weight is wrong for many queries. Ranks have no units. RRF needs no tuning. A tuned weighted sum can win, but only with enough labelled data. <code>lab/retrieve.py:24-33</code>" },
      { q: "Why is a cross-encoder more precise, and why not use it for everything?", a: "It reads question and chunk together, so it sees word-to-word links. But it can't pre-compute anything: one model call per (question, chunk) pair. That cost only fits a small pool, so use it after a cheap wide retrieval. <code>lab/retrieve.py:43-47</code>" },
      { q: "When is a reranker NOT worth it?", a: "When recall is the problem. A reranker only reorders the pool. If the right chunk isn't in the top 20-50, fix retrieval first. <code>lessons/02_hybrid_rerank/run.py:16-17</code>" },
      { q: "What would you do at scale?", a: "ANN (HNSW) in a vector store with built-in hybrid (Qdrant, Milvus, OpenSearch). Metadata filters before ranking to shrink the pool. Cache frequent queries. Rerank only the top 20-50 on GPU. <code>lab/index.py:21-24</code>" },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "03", title: "Multi-hop retrieval",
    job: "Answer questions that need two or more facts.", entry: "lessons/03_multi_hop/run.py:44", uses: ["01", "02", "08"],
    cards: [
      { fp: true, t: "Some facts depend on other facts", b: "Search finds text similar to the <b>query</b>. But in \"error -1021: what window, and how large can it be?\", the second fact (recvWindow max) is similar to the <i>first fact</i>, not to the question.<br><b>So:</b> sometimes you must learn fact 1 before you can ask for fact 2." },
      { t: "Independent vs dependent", b: "<b>Independent</b> sub-questions: split up front and search in parallel (<b>decomposition</b>). Low latency.<br><b>Dependent</b>: search, read, then write the next query from what you found (<b>iterative multi-hop</b>). Must be sequential. <code>lessons/03_multi_hop/run.py:38-56</code>" },
      { t: "Try the cheap fix first", b: "A bigger k widens the net and sometimes catches the second fact. It costs tokens, but no extra round trip.<br><b>Evidence:</b> k=5 3/5, k=10 4/5 = decomposition 4/5. Iterative 5/5. <code>lessons/03_multi_hop/run.py:59-62</code>" },
      { t: "Why a loop needs limits", b: "The model decides when it is \"done\", and it can be wrong. It can repeat itself or drift.<br><b>So:</b> max hops (3), explicit done signal, don't re-add seen chunks, keep short notes instead of full history.<br>It stopped early when one hop was enough: 2.4 calls, not 4. <code>lessons/03_multi_hop/run.py:44-56</code>" },
      { t: "Errors compound", b: "A wrong hop 1 sends hop 2 to the wrong place. The model also leaks its own beliefs into queries (\"recvWindow default 5000 maximum 60000\"). A wrong belief steers the search.<br><b>So:</b> still ground the final answer in sources. <code>lessons/03_multi_hop/run.py:17-20</code>" },
    ],
    qa: [
      { q: "Why does one search fail on multi-hop questions?", a: "Similarity search finds text close to the query vector. A two-fact question is a mix of both facts, so it is close to neither. And when fact 2 is only findable once you know fact 1, no single query can reach it. <code>lab/evals.py:28-30</code>" },
      { q: "Decomposition or an iterative loop? How do you decide?", a: "Ask: does sub-question 2 need the answer to sub-question 1? No -> decompose and run in parallel (fast). Yes -> iterate (sequential), with max hops, a budget, and a clear stop signal. <code>lessons/03_multi_hop/run.py:38-56</code>" },
      { q: "Before building a loop, what cheap fix would you try, and why might it work?", a: "Raise k. The second fact is often just below the cut-off. More context costs tokens but no extra LLM round trip. Here k=10 matched decomposition. <code>lessons/03_multi_hop/run.py:59-60</code>" },
      { q: "How do you evaluate multi-hop retrieval?", a: "Were ALL needed facts retrieved (every hop), not just one? Is the answer correct vs a reference (with a calibrated judge)? What did it cost: calls, tokens, latency? Always compare to the cheap baseline. <code>lessons/03_multi_hop/run.py:66-70</code>" },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "04", title: "CRAG",
    job: "Grade the retrieved chunks before the answer.", entry: "lessons/04_crag/run.py:35", uses: ["01", "02", "08"],
    cards: [
      { fp: true, t: "Garbage in, confident garbage out", b: "Retrieval is a guess. The generator trusts whatever it gets and writes a fluent answer anyway.<br><b>So:</b> check the input <i>before</i> using it. If it is bad, fix it (rewrite, search elsewhere) or say \"not found\"." },
      { t: "The flow", b: "retrieve -> grade each chunk<br>- some relevant -> answer from those only<br>- none -> rewrite query, try another source, grade again (max 2)<br>- still none -> \"Not in the sources\"<br>Paper labels: correct / ambiguous / incorrect. <code>lessons/04_crag/run.py:35-49</code>" },
      { t: "The grader is a model too", b: "Its mistakes limit the gain. It accepted chunks about HMAC <i>signing</i> (same words) but rejected \"HMAC is deprecated\" (the real answer).<br><b>Same topic != answers the question.</b> Grader precision is the whole game. <code>lessons/04_crag/run.py:15-28</code>" },
      { t: "Value depends on how often retrieval fails", b: "naive 22/25, CRAG 23/25, at 2.2x calls.<br>On clean docs with a strict grounding prompt, retrieval rarely fails, so a checker has little to fix. Expect more gain on messy data: duplicates, old pages, far-off wording. <code>lessons/04_crag/run.py:52-66</code>" },
      { t: "Why \"not found\" is a feature", b: "The two errors have different costs. A confident wrong answer about fees or limits can lose a user money. \"I can't find it, contact support\" costs a little time.<br><b>So:</b> track refusals on unanswerable AND wrong refusals on answerable. <code>lessons/04_crag/run.py:49</code> <code>lab/judge.py:11-12</code>" },
    ],
    qa: [
      { q: "What problem does CRAG solve, from first principles?", a: "The generator can't tell good context from bad, and it writes fluently either way. So you put a check between retrieval and generation. Keep the good chunks, fix the retrieval when they are bad, and refuse when nothing good is found. <code>lessons/04_crag/run.py:35-49</code>" },
      { q: "Why did CRAG help so little here?", a: "A checker only helps when there is something to catch. Clean docs + good retriever + strict grounding prompt = few bad inputs. And the grader has its own errors (same topic vs answers the question). <code>lessons/04_crag/run.py:31-32</code>" },
      { q: "CRAG vs Self-RAG?", a: "CRAG checks the INPUT: an external grader around retrieval. Self-RAG checks the OUTPUT: the generator reflects on its own claims. Different failure points, so you can combine them. <code>lessons/04_crag/run.py:25-28</code> <code>lab/verify.py:14-19</code>" },
      { q: "How do you make the grader cheaper, and how do you know it's good?", a: "Cheaper: small classifier or cross-encoder threshold; grade only when the top score is low; batch in one call. Good: hand-label (question, chunk) pairs, measure precision (noise let in) and recall (good chunks rejected). <code>lessons/04_crag/run.py:25-28</code>" },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "05", title: "Self-RAG",
    job: "Check each claim of a draft, then fix the gaps.", entry: "lessons/05_self_rag/run.py:33", uses: ["01", "06", "08"],
    cards: [
      { fp: true, t: "Checking is easier than writing", b: "It is hard to write a perfect answer in one go. It is easier to check one claim against one source.<br><b>So:</b> draft, split into claims, verify each, then fix only the gaps. That is Self-RAG's core." },
      { t: "Reflection questions", b: "<b>Retrieve</b>: do I need to search?<br><b>IsRel</b>: is this passage relevant?<br><b>IsSup</b>: is my claim supported?<br><b>IsUse</b>: does it answer the question?<br>The paper fine-tunes these; we approximate with prompts. <code>lab/verify.py:5-11</code>" },
      { t: "A model doesn't know what it doesn't know", b: "Adaptive retrieval asks the model \"do you need to search?\". It said no on <b>7/35</b> doc questions and answered from memory, wrongly.<br><b>So:</b> for a domain assistant, always retrieve. Skipping turns it back into a plain chatbot. <code>lessons/05_self_rag/run.py:35-36</code>" },
      { t: "Two different failures", b: "<b>Saying false things</b> (hallucination) vs <b>leaving out true things</b> (incomplete).<br>Baseline was 35/35 grounded yet missed 2: no false claims, but a needed fact was missing.<br><b>So:</b> measure groundedness AND completeness. <code>lab/verify.py:14-19</code>" },
      { t: "Result: the fix worked, the skip hurt", b: "baseline 33/35 (1 call) vs self-RAG 31/35 (3.3 calls).<br>Verify-and-fix repaired 2 incomplete answers; adaptive retrieval lost more than that. <code>lessons/05_self_rag/run.py:33-57</code>" },
    ],
    qa: [
      { q: "Why does verify-then-fix work better than one careful draft?", a: "Checking one claim against one source is a small, clear task. Writing a full correct answer is a big, open task. Splitting the big task into small checks catches errors the writer missed. <code>lab/verify.py:5-19</code>" },
      { q: "Would you use adaptive retrieval?", a: "Rarely. The router is the same model, and it can't see its own knowledge gaps. It skipped 7/35 doc questions. Default to always retrieving; skip only clear small talk, and test the router on its own labelled set. <code>lessons/05_self_rag/run.py:35-36</code>" },
      { q: "Grounded but wrong - how is that possible?", a: "Groundedness only checks what IS said. Every claim can be supported while a needed fact is missing. Pair it with a correctness or completeness metric. <code>lab/verify.py:18</code>" },
      { q: "How do you stop a reflect loop?", a: "Stop when every claim is supported and the question is answered. Else a hard cap (2 rounds) and a budget. Then return the last verified state or refuse. Never loop until the model \"feels\" happy. <code>lessons/05_self_rag/run.py:40-47</code>" },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "06", title: "Agent loop + tools",
    job: "Let the model pick tools in a loop.", entry: "lessons/06_agent_loop/run.py:18", uses: ["01", "02", "08"],
    cards: [
      { fp: true, t: "A model can only write text", b: "It can't fetch a price or search an index by itself. The runtime must do it for the model. The next step often depends on the result.<br><b>So:</b> a loop. Model asks -> runtime runs the tool -> result goes back -> repeat." },
      { t: "The loop", b: "<code>repeat (max N):<br>&nbsp; resp = LLM(messages, tools)<br>&nbsp; if no tool call: return resp<br>&nbsp; run each tool, append result</code><br>ReAct (reason -> act -> observe) with native function calling. ~20 lines. <code>lessons/06_agent_loop/run.py:18-32</code>" },
      { t: "The schema is the whole interface", b: "The model never sees your code. It sees only the name, description, and parameters.<br><b>So:</b> one clear job per tool, say <i>when</i> to use it, typed params, compact output. <code>lab/tools.py:38-58</code>" },
      { t: "The model reacts only to what's in context", b: "If a tool crashes the loop, the model learns nothing. If it returns \"symbol not found; check the name\" as text, the model can recover.<br><b>Evidence:</b> FAKECOINUSDT failed; it searched the error code and explained it. <code>lab/tools.py:61-68</code>" },
      { t: "Who decides to stop?", b: "The model stops when it returns no tool call. That is a model decision, so it can fail.<br><b>So:</b> a step limit is the only thing that <i>guarantees</i> termination. <code>lessons/06_agent_loop/run.py:21-24</code>" },
      { t: "Evidence", b: "Multi-hop 5/5 at 2.2 steps. Same as the hand-written loop in lesson 3, with less code. Parallel tool calls in one step. <code>lessons/06_agent_loop/run.py:54-59</code>" },
    ],
    qa: [
      { q: "Why do agents need a loop at all?", a: "The model only writes text; the runtime has to act for it. And the next action depends on the last result, so you can't plan every call up front. Hence: call model, run tools, feed results back, repeat until no tool call or a limit. <code>lessons/06_agent_loop/run.py:18-32</code>" },
      { q: "How do you design good tools?", a: "The schema is all the model sees. So: few tools with one job each; names and descriptions that say when to use them; typed params; compact structured output; errors the model can act on; idempotent writes, kept apart from reads so you can put permissions on them. <code>lab/tools.py:46-58</code>" },
      { q: "RAG as a pipeline vs retrieval as a tool?", a: "Pipeline: code decides - always retrieve once. Predictable and cheap. Tool: the model decides when and what to search, and can mix sources. Flexible, but less predictable, so it needs limits, traces, and trajectory evals. <code>lab/rag.py:12-15</code> <code>lab/tools.py:18-20</code>" },
      { q: "What breaks in agent loops, and why?", a: "Every stop/choice is a model guess: infinite loops (cap steps), repeated identical calls (detect), hallucinated args (validate schema). Context is finite: huge outputs flood it (truncate). Tool text is read like instructions: injection (lesson 7). <code>lessons/06_agent_loop/run.py:21-31</code>" },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "07", title: "Agent harness",
    job: "Make the loop safe: hooks, checkpoints, budget.", entry: "lab/agent.py:90", uses: ["01", "06"],
    cards: [
      { fp: true, t: "Must-hold rules can't live in the model", b: "A model is probabilistic, and its input can manipulate it. A prompt rule holds <i>most</i> of the time.<br><b>So:</b> anything that must ALWAYS hold (permissions, limits, saving state) goes into deterministic code around the model. That code is the harness." },
      { t: "Permissions: the hook decides", b: "The model chose to call <code>place_order</code> every time. The pre-tool hook denied unapproved orders and orders over 1,000 USDT.<br>The prompt can ask nicely; the hook decides. <code>lessons/07_harness/run.py:14-23</code> <code>lab/agent.py:79-84</code>" },
      { t: "Injection: one text stream", b: "The model reads instructions and data in the same stream. It can't reliably tell them apart.<br><b>So:</b> treat tool output as untrusted data, and keep privileged actions behind hooks. Then even a hijacked model can't act (defence in depth). <code>lessons/07_harness/run.py:26-33</code>" },
      { t: "Crashes: save after every step", b: "Processes crash, and LLM calls cost money.<br><b>So:</b> checkpoint after every step with an atomic write (temp file + rename). Resume by session id. Side-effect tools need idempotency keys, so a trade doesn't run twice. <code>lab/agent.py:47-51</code> <code>lab/agent.py:114</code>" },
      { t: "Context is finite", b: "Every step adds tokens, and quality drops as context grows (\"context rot\"). So you must choose what stays.<br>none: 6 steps, 51k tokens. clear: <b>no answer</b> (forgot, looped). notes then clear: <b>4 steps, 12k</b>. Keep the facts, drop the raw output. <code>lab/agent.py:53-77</code>" },
      { t: "Prompt cache: stable prefix", b: "Providers reuse work for an identical prompt prefix.<br><b>So:</b> keep system + tools fixed and append at the end. Any edit to early context breaks the cache after that point. <code>lab/agent.py:58-59</code> <code>lab/agent.py:102</code>" },
    ],
    qa: [
      { q: "What is an agent harness, and why can't the model do its job?", a: "The code around the model: loop, tool execution, checkpoints, hooks, context management, budgets, tracing. The model is probabilistic and can be manipulated, so rules that must always hold have to be enforced by deterministic code. <code>lab/agent.py:90-118</code>" },
      { q: "How do you stop an agent doing something dangerous?", a: "Enforce it outside the model: pre-tool hooks for permissions, limits, and human approval; credentials scoped to the minimum (Agent OS: isolated sub-accounts, no external withdrawals). The prompt asks; the hook decides. <code>lab/agent.py:79-88</code>" },
      { q: "Why is prompt injection hard to fix inside the model?", a: "Instructions and data arrive in the same text stream, and the model has no hard boundary between them. So defend outside it: mark tool output as untrusted, limit tools per task, gate privileged actions, log, and test with injection cases. <code>lessons/07_harness/run.py:88-94</code>" },
      { q: "How do you manage context in long-running agents?", a: "Context is finite and quality drops as it grows. Truncate outputs at the source; clear old results but keep a short notes message so facts survive; use memory files; hand sub-tasks to subagents. Remember edits to early context break prompt caching. <code>lab/agent.py:53-77</code>" },
      { q: "How do you recover a session after a crash?", a: "Checkpoint full state (messages, step, budget) after every step with an atomic write. Load by session id and continue. Make side-effect tools idempotent or record started/finished, so a resume doesn't repeat a trade. <code>lab/agent.py:38-51</code>" },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "08", title: "Evals",
    job: "Measure: judge, reliability, regression gate.", entry: "lessons/08_evals/run.py:30", uses: ["01", "06", "07"],
    cards: [
      { fp: true, t: "A number is only as good as how you got it", b: "You can't improve what you can't measure. But every measurement has an <b>instrument</b> (the judge), a <b>sample</b> (the golden set), and <b>noise</b> (random outputs).<br><b>So:</b> check each of the three before you trust a score." },
      { t: "Instrument: judge the judge", b: "An LLM judge is a model, so it has errors and biases (position, length, self-preference).<br><b>So:</b> hand-label a set, measure agreement / Cohen's kappa. strict 0.94 (kappa 0.89), rubric 1.00 on 18 labels. Caveat: same author wrote labels and rubric. <code>lessons/08_evals/run.py:22-39</code>" },
      { t: "Noise: one run is one sample", b: "Agents are random. <b>pass@k</b>: at least one of k works (fine when you can retry). <b>pass^k</b>: all k work - what users feel.<br>Independent tries multiply: 0.9^5 ≈ <b>0.59</b>. <code>lessons/08_evals/run.py:54-58</code>" },
      { t: "Sample: a test everyone passes says nothing", b: "pass@1 = pass@3 = pass^3 = 1.00 means the benchmark is saturated. It can't tell a good change from a bad one.<br><b>So:</b> add harder cases until the best system scores 60-80%. <code>lessons/08_evals/run.py:56-60</code>" },
      { t: "Levels: find where it broke", b: "<b>Retrieval</b> (hit@k, MRR) -> <b>answer</b> (correct, grounded, refuses) -> <b>end to end</b> (success, latency, cost) -> <b>trajectory</b> (steps, repeats, unsafe actions).<br>Each level points to a different part to fix. <code>lab/evals.py:33-48</code> <code>lessons/08_evals/run.py:47-51</code>" },
      { t: "Regression gate", b: "Every change can break something you didn't touch.<br><b>So:</b> save metrics in <code>baseline.json</code>, compare on every prompt/model/tool change, exit 1 if a metric drops beyond tolerance (0.02 for deterministic). <code>lessons/08_evals/run.py:72-85</code>" },
    ],
    qa: [
      { q: "How do you evaluate a RAG or agent system?", a: "In layers, so a failure points to a part: retrieval (recall@k, MRR), answer (correctness, groundedness, refusal), end to end (success, latency, cost), trajectory (steps, repeats, unsafe actions). Offline in CI on every change; online feedback turns failures into new cases. <code>lessons/08_evals/run.py:47-51</code> <code>lessons/08_evals/run.py:67-70</code>" },
      { q: "Can you trust LLM-as-judge? Why or why not?", a: "It is a model, so it is an instrument with its own errors. Trust it only after calibration: hand-label, measure kappa, read disagreements, fix the rubric, test on a held-out set. Human-human agreement is the ceiling. <code>lab/judge.py:5-20</code>" },
      { q: "Why does pass^k drop so fast?", a: "All k tries must succeed, and independent tries multiply: p^k. 90% per try -> pass^5 ≈ 59%. For a user-facing agent, that consistency is what users feel. <code>lessons/08_evals/run.py:57-58</code>" },
      { q: "Your benchmark scores 100%. Good news?", a: "No: it carries no information now. It can't show if a change helped or hurt. Add harder cases (3+ hops, conflicting sources, live data, injection) until the best system scores 60-80%. <code>lessons/08_evals/run.py:60</code>" },
      { q: "How do you build a benchmark for a new domain?", a: "Real user questions and production failures; labelled sources and answers with guidelines; edge cases (unanswerable, multi-hop, adversarial); versioned; held-out split; check contamination; keep it hard. <code>lab/evals.py:13-21</code>" },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "09", title: "Durable execution",
    job: "Survive a crash: event log, replay, retries.", entry: "lessons/09_durable/run.py:31", uses: ["01", "06"],
    cards: [
      { fp: true, t: "A process can die at any line", b: "A long agent run makes many slow, paid calls. Some move money. The process can die between any two of them. Memory is lost, but the outside world is not undone.<br><b>So:</b> write each finished step to disk. After a crash, run the same code again and skip what is done. That is durable execution, the core idea of Temporal." },
      { t: "Why log the history, not the state?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> lesson 7 saved the message list. That fits one loop shape only.<br><b>So:</b> log each step result. Run the code again; finished steps return the logged result (replay).<br><b>Evidence (from the code):</b> crash after search:0. Run 2 replays 2 steps and runs 2. Run 3 replays 4, runs 0. <code>lab/durable.py:43-49</code> <code>lessons/09_durable/run.py:59-74</code>" },
      { t: "Why must side effects go through step()?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> replay runs the workflow code again from the top. A direct LLM call would run again, cost again, and may answer differently.<br><b>So:</b> the workflow holds only <code>ctx.step()</code> calls and pure logic. <code>lessons/09_durable/run.py:31-35</code>" },
      { t: "Why fsync before going on?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a write can wait in an OS buffer. A crash then loses a step the code thinks is done.<br><b>So:</b> append, flush, fsync, and only then continue.<br><b>Cost:</b> each step waits for the disk. <code>lab/durable.py:61-65</code>" },
      { t: "Why retries with backoff?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> networks fail for a short time. One timeout should not fail a workflow and waste the earlier steps.<br><b>So:</b> retry inside the step and wait longer each time (0.2 s, 0.4 s).<br><b>Evidence (from the code):</b> a tool fails twice. retries=1: workflow fails. retries=3: works on attempt 3. <code>lab/durable.py:50-58</code> <code>lessons/09_durable/run.py:77-93</code>" },
      { t: "At least once, not exactly once", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a crash can come after the side effect but before the log write. The log says \"not done\", so the step runs again.<br><b>So:</b> send an idempotency key that is the same on every replay. The receiver drops repeats.<br><b>Evidence (from the code):</b> no key: 2 orders. With key: 1. <code>lab/durable.py:37-39</code> <code>lessons/09_durable/run.py:96-115</code>" },
      { t: "Why must the workflow be deterministic?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> replay matches the log by position. If new code asks for steps in another order, a logged result goes to the wrong step.<br><b>So:</b> check the step name and raise <code>NonDeterminism</code>.<br><b>Cost:</b> you can't freely change a workflow that has unfinished runs. <code>lab/durable.py:43-46</code> <code>lessons/09_durable/run.py:38-42</code>" },
      { t: "The same idea in Temporal", b: "<code>ctx.step(name, fn)</code> becomes an <b>activity</b>. The function that calls steps becomes a <b>workflow</b>. The log file becomes the history on the server.<br><b>Evidence:</b> a worker was killed inside <code>search</code>. A second worker finished the run: <code>plan</code> ran once (its result was in the history), the killed <code>search</code> ran twice. <code>lessons/09_durable/temporal_run.py:59-69</code> <code>lessons/09_durable/temporal_run.py:42-48</code>" },
      { t: "How does the server know a worker died?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a dead worker sends nothing. Silence looks the same as slow work.<br><b>So:</b> each activity has a timeout; when it passes, the server gives the activity to another worker.<br><b>Evidence:</b> the run lost 30 s waiting for that timeout.<br><b>Fix:</b> long activities send a heartbeat, with a short heartbeat timeout. <code>lessons/09_durable/temporal_run.py:63-66</code> <code>lessons/09_durable/README.md:85-90</code>" },
      { t: "Why keep step results small?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> the log is read again on every replay, and it only grows.<br><b>So:</b> big results (documents, tool output) go to a blob store; the log keeps a reference. Very long runs start a fresh history with the current state (continue-as-new). <code>lab/durable.py:29-33</code> <code>lessons/09_durable/README.md:101-110</code>" },
      { t: "Waiting for a human costs nothing", b: "<b>Problem:</b> an approval can take days. A process that waits for it can't be restarted.<br><b>So:</b> the workflow stops at a wait; its history is on the server. A signal (the approval) wakes it, on any worker. <code>lessons/09_durable/README.md:101-110</code>" },
    ],
    qa: [
      { q: "What is durable execution, from first principles?", a: "A process can die at any line, and a long run has many costly steps. So record each finished step on disk, and rebuild the state after a crash by running the same code again: done steps return their logged result, real work starts at the first step with no result. Trade-off: the code must be deterministic. <code>lab/durable.py:41-49</code>" },
      { q: "Save the state (lesson 7) or log the history (lesson 9)?", a: "Both exist because memory is lost in a crash. State: simple, but tied to one loop shape. History: works for any plain function and gives a full record, but the code must replay the same way and replay gets longer as the log grows. <code>lab/agent.py:47-51</code> <code>lab/durable.py:61-65</code>" },
      { q: "Why can't a step run exactly once?", a: "Doing the side effect and writing the log are two actions. A crash can fall between them, and no code can make them one action across two systems. So accept at-least-once, and make repeats harmless with an idempotency key. <code>lab/durable.py:59-65</code> <code>lab/durable.py:37-39</code>" },
      { q: "What breaks when you change workflow code?", a: "Old logs were written by the old step order. Replay matches by position, so new code can ask for a step the log does not have there. This lab raises NonDeterminism. In production you version the workflow or let old runs finish on old code. <code>lessons/09_durable/run.py:38-42</code> <code>lessons/09_durable/run.py:117-128</code>" },
      { q: "What must not be inside workflow code?", a: "Anything that can give a different result on replay or that touches the outside world: LLM calls, tool calls, file or network I/O. Put each one in a step, so replay reads its result from the log. <code>lessons/09_durable/run.py:31-35</code>" },
      { q: "When is a workflow engine too much?", a: "It exists because long runs die in the middle. A short request with no side effects does not have that problem: the client can just retry. Use an engine when a run is long, costly to repeat, waits for humans or timers, or has side effects that must not happen twice. Trade-off: deterministic code and one more system to run. <code>lessons/09_durable/README.md:101-110</code>" },
      { q: "How does Temporal map to the engine built here?", a: "Same root idea: log each step, replay after a crash. <code>ctx.step</code> is an activity, the function that calls steps is a workflow, the log file is the history on the server, a second call is a worker replaying. Temporal adds many workers, task queues, timers and signals. <code>lessons/09_durable/temporal_run.py:59-69</code> <code>lab/durable.py:41-49</code>" },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "10", title: "LLM gateway",
    job: "One door to all LLM providers: failover, breaker, structured output.", entry: "lab/gateway.py:73", uses: ["06"],
    cards: [
      { fp: true, t: "Every step depends on someone else's API", b: "An agent step is a call to a provider you do not control. It is slow, rate limited, sometimes down, and each provider has its own request fields.<br><b>So:</b> put one gateway between all agents and all providers. Failover, retries, limits and provider differences live in one place." },
      { t: "Why are retries not enough?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a retry asks the same provider again. That helps with a short error, not with an outage.<br><b>So:</b> a chain of providers; move on when one fails.<br><b>Evidence:</b> flaky provider: 9/20 -> 18/20 with retries -> 20/20 with fallback. Provider down: retries 0/20, fallback 20/20. <code>lab/gateway.py:79-100</code> <code>lessons/10_gateway/run.py:27-35</code>" },
      { t: "Why not retry every error?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a 400 or 401 means the request itself is wrong. It will fail again, on every provider.<br><b>So:</b> only timeouts, 429 and 5xx are retried or passed to the next provider. The rest fail at once.<br><b>Cost:</b> you must keep the list right for each provider. <code>lab/gateway.py:24-26</code>" },
      { t: "Why a circuit breaker?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> fallback still tries the dead provider first and pays its timeout on every request.<br><b>So:</b> after N failures in a row, skip it for a cooldown, then test it again.<br><b>Evidence:</b> 10 requests, primary hangs 2 s: 20 s lost without a breaker, 4 s with. <code>lab/gateway.py:81-98</code> <code>lessons/10_gateway/run.py:38-46</code>" },
      { t: "Why validate the model's output?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> the model's text is input to your code. A prompt is a wish, not a contract.<br><b>So:</b> parse, validate against a schema, send the error back for a repair round.<br><b>Evidence:</b> loose prompt 0/10 valid, 10/10 after repair. Schema in the prompt: 10/10 first try. But valid is not correct: one run gave price 0.0 for a market order. <code>lab/gateway.py:105-115</code>" },
      { t: "Routing: measure before you build it", b: "<b>Idea:</b> cheap model first, strong model only when needed.<br><b>Evidence:</b> cheap model 12/12, strong model 12/12 with 25-40% more tokens. The cascade never called the strong model and still cost double. Latency changed more between two runs than between the models.<br><b>So:</b> route only the slice of traffic where the cheap model fails. Find it with evals first. <code>lessons/10_gateway/run.py:85-105</code>" },
      { t: "Why does thinking hurt the first token?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> users feel the time to the first token, not the total. A model that thinks first shows nothing while it thinks.<br><b>Evidence:</b> thinking on: first visible token after 7-12 s, the rest in 0.5 s. Thinking off: 0.6-0.8 s.<br><b>So:</b> turn thinking off for simple steps, or show the thinking as progress. <code>lab/gateway.py:117-122</code> <code>lessons/10_gateway/run.py:157-167</code>" },
      { t: "A 200 is not a good answer", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a call can succeed and still give nothing useful.<br><b>Evidence:</b> with <code>max_tokens=5</code> the provider returned HTTP 200 and empty content: the thinking used the whole budget.<br><b>So:</b> check the content, not only the status. Put 'empty answer' in the evals. <code>lessons/10_gateway/README.md:100-107</code> <code>lessons/10_gateway/run.py:16-23</code>" },
      { t: "Why one option name for all providers?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> each provider has its own request fields. Thinking off is <code>thinking: disabled</code> on one and <code>reasoning: enabled false</code> on another.<br><b>So:</b> callers say <code>think=False</code>; the gateway maps it per provider. A provider change does not touch agent code. <code>lab/gateway.py:61-70</code> <code>lab/gateway.py:50-52</code>" },
      { t: "Why jitter in the backoff?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> after an error, all clients wait the same time and retry together. The provider gets hit in waves.<br><b>So:</b> multiply each wait by a random factor (0.5 to 1.5). The retries spread out. <code>lab/gateway.py:98-99</code>" },
    ],
    qa: [
      { q: "Why put a gateway between agents and LLM providers?", a: "Each step depends on an API you do not control. If every agent handles failover, limits and provider quirks alone, you solve it N times. One gateway gives one place for failover, retries, keys, quotas, cost and provider differences. Trade-off: one more hop, and it must not become a single point of failure. <code>lab/gateway.py:73-100</code>" },
      { q: "Retry or fall back - when do you use which?", a: "A retry repeats the same call, so it only fixes errors that go away: a timeout, a 429, a 503. When errors continue, the provider is down and only another provider helps. Never retry a 400 or 401. In the lab retries gave 18/20 on a flaky provider and 0/20 in an outage. <code>lab/gateway.py:24-26</code> <code>lessons/10_gateway/run.py:27-35</code>" },
      { q: "How do you get reliable structured output?", a: "The model's text is untrusted input. So: put the schema in the prompt, parse, validate with a real validator, and send the error back for a limited number of repair rounds. Then test correctness with evals, because a valid object can still be wrong. Trade-off: each repair is another call. <code>lab/gateway.py:105-115</code> <code>lessons/10_gateway/run.py:132-154</code>" },
      { q: "A provider fails in the middle of a stream. What do you do?", a: "Before the first token you can switch and nobody sees it. After it, the user has seen text, so a switch would show. Either restart and tell the client to drop the partial text, or buffer on the server and accept a later first token. <code>lab/gateway.py:117-122</code>" },
      { q: "How do you route between a cheap and a strong model?", a: "The reason to route is that the cheap model fails on some tasks. So first measure where. Then route by rule (task type), by a small classifier, or by a cascade (cheap first, escalate when unsure). In the lab the cheap model scored 12/12, so routing only added cost. Trade-off: the router is one more thing that can be wrong. <code>lessons/10_gateway/run.py:85-105</code>" },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "11", title: "Memory and state",
    job: "Keep facts about a user between sessions.", entry: "lab/memory.py:25", uses: ["01"],
    cards: [
      { fp: true, t: "The model forgets everything between calls", b: "A model keeps nothing. What it knows about a user must be put into the context again on every call, and the context is limited.<br><b>So:</b> memory is a selection problem, like RAG: of all that was ever said, which few lines go into this call?" },
      { t: "Three layers, three lifetimes", b: "<b>Working memory:</b> the context of one run.<br><b>Session state:</b> progress of one conversation (step, budget, approvals).<br><b>Long-term memory:</b> facts that outlive the session.<br>Each has its own lifetime and its own store. Mixing them is the common mistake. <code>lab/agent.py:47-51</code> <code>lab/memory.py:25-30</code>" },
      { t: "Why not keep the full history?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> it grows with every session and is paid on every call.<br><b>Evidence:</b> full history 12/12 at 271 tokens. Extracted facts 12/12 at 110 tokens, and that stays flat.<br><b>Honest note:</b> at this size full history is fine. Start with it; switch when it stops being cheap. <code>lessons/11_memory/run.py:33-44</code>" },
      { t: "Why does append-only memory fail?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> users change their mind. If you only add, the old and the new fact both come back.<br><b>Evidence:</b> 9/12. All 3 misses were changed facts (EUR -> USD, UTC+8 -> UTC+1, limit 50 -> 200).<br><b>So:</b> the writer sees the existing memories and replaces by id: 12/12. <code>lab/memory.py:35-50</code>" },
      { t: "Why must scope be in the store?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> one shared pool mixes users.<br><b>Evidence:</b> without scope: 7/12. One user got another user's currency, and one user's update overwrote another user's order limit.<br><b>So:</b> every row has a user; reads and updates filter by it, in code. <code>lab/memory.py:32-33</code> <code>lab/memory.py:43-49</code>" },
      { t: "Memory is written from untrusted text", b: "<b>Problem:</b> an injected instruction that lands in memory comes back in every later session.<br><b>So:</b> limits and permissions stay in code (lesson 7 hooks), never in memory alone. Keep the source of each fact so you can explain and delete it. <code>lab/memory.py:14-22</code>" },
      { t: "The window has a fixed budget: order it", b: "<b>Problem:</b> memory, documents, history and tools all want space, and a change early in the prompt breaks the prompt cache for everything after it.<br><b>So:</b> stable parts first (system prompt, tools), then memories, documents, recent turns. Give each part a share. <code>lessons/11_memory/README.md:73-85</code>" },
      { t: "Why write memory after the session?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> the write needs an LLM call. Inside the answer path it adds latency to every reply.<br><b>So:</b> write in the background when the session ends (a queue job). The cost: a fact from this session is not in memory until the job has run. <code>lessons/11_memory/README.md:87-96</code> <code>lessons/11_memory/run.py:23-30</code>" },
    ],
    qa: [
      { q: "What kinds of memory does an agent have?", a: "The model keeps nothing, so every kind is something you put back into the context. Working memory: the window of one run. Session state: the run's progress, in a checkpoint or workflow history. Long-term memory: facts, past events, how-to, in a store per user. They differ in lifetime, so they need different stores. <code>lab/memory.py:25-30</code>" },
      { q: "What is the main failure of a simple memory store?", a: "Stale facts. An append-only store returns the old and the new fact together, and the model picks one or gives up (9/12 in the lab). The write step must update: show the writer the existing memories and let it replace by id. Trade-off: the write needs an LLM call that can itself be wrong. <code>lab/memory.py:35-50</code>" },
      { q: "How do you keep memory safe with many tenants?", a: "The model must never choose whose memory it reads. Scope every row by tenant and user, and apply the filter in the store query from the authenticated identity. Without it the lab leaked a preference and let one user overwrite another's limit. <code>lab/memory.py:32-33</code> <code>lab/memory.py:52-57</code>" },
      { q: "When is full history better than extracted memory?", a: "When it is small. It loses nothing and needs no extra LLM call; in the lab it scored 12/12. Extraction pays off when history grows past what you want to pay per call. Trade-off of extraction: the writer can drop or change a fact. <code>lessons/11_memory/run.py:33-44</code>" },
      { q: "Where do state and memory live in a scaled runtime?", a: "Workers must be free to die, so they hold nothing. Session state sits in the workflow engine or a fast key-value store, keyed by session id, so any worker can continue. Long-term memory sits in a database with a vector index, partitioned by tenant. Trade-off: every step pays a read. <code>docs/design/agent-runtime.md:101-110</code>" },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "12", title: "Multi-agent",
    job: "A supervisor that calls worker agents as tools.", entry: "lab/multi.py:28", uses: ["06"],
    cards: [
      { fp: true, t: "One agent has one context window", b: "Everything an agent reads stays in its window, and quality drops as the window fills.<br><b>So:</b> a second agent is a second, clean window: give it a sub-task, get back only the result. That is the only real reason for multi-agent. The price: every handoff can lose or change information." },
      { t: "Agents as tools", b: "<b>Idea:</b> a worker is a normal agent loop. To the supervisor it looks like a tool with one argument: the task.<br>The supervisor sees only the worker's final answer, not its searches. <code>lab/multi.py:33-42</code> <code>lab/loop.py:18-43</code>" },
      { t: "The single agent won", b: "<b>Evidence:</b> single agent 8/8 at 2.2 LLM calls and 4.7k tokens. Supervisor + 2 workers: 7/8 at 10-14 calls and 36-58k tokens.<br><b>Why:</b> the tasks fit in one window, so a second window buys nothing and each handoff costs. <code>lessons/12_multi_agent/run.py:61-66</code>" },
      { t: "The failures were coordination failures", b: "<b>Evidence:</b> the supervisor delegated again and again until its step limit. A worker hit its own limit and the supervisor then said 'Not in the sources'. One answer was a report about delegating.<br>None were knowledge failures: the single agent got those questions right. <code>lab/multi.py:44-50</code>" },
      { t: "Budgets multiply down the tree", b: "<b>Problem:</b> each limit looks small. 6 supervisor steps x 2 workers x 4 steps = up to 54 LLM calls for one question.<br><b>So:</b> one budget for the whole tree (tokens, calls, time), passed down and counted at every level. <code>lab/multi.py:29-31</code>" },
      { t: "Short briefs did better here", b: "<b>Expected:</b> a 4-word brief loses context.<br><b>Evidence:</b> it scored 8/8 at a quarter of the tokens. Workers did one lookup each and the supervisor kept the reasoning.<br><b>So:</b> a thin handoff fails only when the worker needs the detail. Measure on your tasks. <code>lab/multi.py:23-25</code> <code>lessons/12_multi_agent/run.py:67-70</code>" },
      { t: "More agents, more variance", b: "<b>Problem:</b> every agent makes its own choices, and choices differ between runs.<br><b>Evidence:</b> same code, same tasks, temperature 0: the team used 14.4 LLM calls per task in one run and 10.5 in the next. The single agent: 2.2 both times.<br><b>So:</b> judge a team over several trials (pass^k). <code>lessons/12_multi_agent/README.md:80-83</code> <code>lessons/12_multi_agent/run.py:45-58</code>" },
      { t: "Supervisor or handoff?", b: "<b>Supervisor:</b> control comes back. Workers are like function calls; the lead writes the answer. Fits independent sub-tasks.<br><b>Handoff:</b> control moves. The next agent owns the conversation. Fits stages (triage, then a specialist).<br>A fixed order A, B, C is a workflow, not a team. <code>lessons/12_multi_agent/README.md:25-30</code> <code>lab/multi.py:33-42</code>" },
      { t: "Parallel workers barely helped here", b: "<b>Expected:</b> workers in parallel make the team faster.<br><b>Evidence:</b> 23 s vs 26 s, 21 s vs 22 s: inside the noise. Few turns had two workers at once, and LLM time dominates. <code>lessons/12_multi_agent/README.md:85-86</code> <code>lab/loop.py:36-38</code>" },
      { t: "Context isolation was small too", b: "<b>Expected:</b> the supervisor's window stays small because workers keep their searches.<br><b>Evidence:</b> supervisor's largest prompt ~2.5-3.2k tokens, single agent ~3.0-3.2k. Workers sent back long answers.<br><b>So:</b> ask workers for a short, structured result. <code>lessons/12_multi_agent/README.md:88-90</code>" },
    ],
    qa: [
      { q: "Why use more than one agent at all?", a: "One agent has one window, and it fills. More agents give a clean window per sub-task, parallel work, and isolation of tools and trust. They do not give more intelligence: it is the same model. In the lab one agent beat the team on score, cost and speed because the tasks fit one window. <code>lab/multi.py:28-50</code>" },
      { q: "What are the typical failure modes of multi-agent systems?", a: "Each handoff is a lossy copy and each agent adds its own decisions. So: lost information at the handoff, delegation loops, budgets that multiply, a worker that fails quietly, duplicate work, and more variance between runs (10.5 vs 14.4 calls on the same tasks). <code>lessons/12_multi_agent/run.py:45-58</code>" },
      { q: "How do you control cost in a tree of agents?", a: "Limits per agent multiply. So use one budget for the whole tree, passed to every worker; step limits at each level; a deadline passed down; and a check for repeated identical delegations. All in code, not in the prompt. <code>lab/multi.py:29-31</code> <code>lab/loop.py:18-21</code>" },
      { q: "When is multi-agent worth it?", a: "When the work does not fit one window: wide tasks with many independent parts that each read a lot. Or when parts need different trust levels or owners. Not for small tasks, tightly coupled steps, or tight latency. <code>lab/multi.py:13-22</code>" },
      { q: "Supervisor or handoff - what is the difference?", a: "Both exist to give a sub-task a clean window. With a supervisor, control returns: workers act like function calls and the lead writes the answer. With a handoff, control moves and the next agent owns the conversation. Supervisor fits independent sub-tasks; handoff fits stages. Trade-off: the supervisor pays for every round trip. <code>lessons/12_multi_agent/README.md:25-30</code>" },
      { q: "How would you evaluate single agent against multi-agent?", a: "A team has more model decisions, so more variance. One run proves nothing. Use the same tasks and judge, several trials, and compare correctness, tokens, LLM calls, latency and the spread between runs. Read the failed runs. Start with one agent; add workers only for task types where it fails. <code>lessons/12_multi_agent/run.py:45-58</code>" },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "13", title: "Event-driven runtime",
    job: "Queue, workers, dead letters, event store.", entry: "lab/bus.py:29", uses: ["09"],
    cards: [
      { fp: true, t: "A run is slow, a request must be fast", b: "An agent run takes seconds to minutes and can fail in the middle. A request that waits for it times out, and the server that holds it can't restart.<br><b>So:</b> split accepting work from doing work. The API writes a job and answers at once. Stateless workers pull jobs. Every change is written as an event." },
      { t: "Why are more workers not always faster?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a worker mostly waits on the provider.<br><b>Evidence:</b> 40 runs: 1 worker 4.0 s, 4 workers 1.0 s, 16 workers 0.3 s. 16 workers behind a provider limit of 4: 1.0 s again.<br><b>So:</b> scale on queue depth and wait, capped by the provider quota. <code>lessons/13_events/run.py:30-44</code>" },
      { t: "A queue adds no capacity", b: "<b>Problem:</b> work arrives faster than it is done. A queue only moves the wait.<br><b>Evidence:</b> no limit: backlog 120, wait p95 2.8 s and growing. Limit 20: p95 0.4 s, half refused with a fast 429.<br><b>So:</b> bound the backlog. Having no limit is not a decision. <code>lessons/13_events/run.py:47-74</code>" },
      { t: "Delivery is at-least-once", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a worker can die after the side effect and before the ack. The job comes back.<br><b>So:</b> the consumer remembers the job ids it has done.<br><b>Evidence:</b> plain consumer: 60 side effects for 50 jobs. Idempotent: 50. <code>lab/bus.py:43-58</code> <code>lessons/13_events/run.py:77-97</code>" },
      { t: "Why a dead-letter queue?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a job that always fails comes back forever and burns tokens each time.<br><b>So:</b> after N attempts, move it aside and alert.<br><b>Evidence:</b> 1 poison job: dead after 3 attempts; the other 9 finished. <code>lab/bus.py:45-48</code> <code>lessons/13_events/run.py:100-113</code>" },
      { t: "State is a fold over events", b: "<b>Idea:</b> store every change as an event. State = apply each event in order.<br><b>Gain:</b> audit log, state at any past point ('what did the agent know then?'), and new views built later (tokens per tenant).<br><b>Cost:</b> long streams need snapshots. <code>lessons/13_events/run.py:117-136</code> <code>lab/bus.py:70-83</code>" },
      { t: "Two workers on one run", b: "<b>Problem:</b> both read version 3, both try to continue.<br><b>So:</b> an append says which version it last saw. The stale one gets <code>Conflict</code>.<br><b>Cost:</b> the loser must read again and retry. <code>lab/bus.py:70-77</code>" },
      { t: "The producer retries too", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a send can time out. The producer can't know if the job was stored, so it sends again.<br><b>So:</b> a dedupe key on send.<br><b>Evidence:</b> 3 sends with the same key made 1 job. <code>lab/bus.py:36-41</code> <code>lessons/13_events/run.py:95-97</code>" },
      { t: "Order matters only inside one run", b: "<b>Problem:</b> one global order for all runs is costly, and nobody needs it.<br><b>So:</b> use the run id as the partition key. Events of one run stay in order; different runs go in parallel. <code>lessons/13_events/README.md:97-102</code> <code>lab/bus.py:79-83</code>" },
      { t: "Saved but not sent", b: "<b>Problem:</b> you write the state to the database, then publish an event. A crash between the two leaves them different.<br><b>So:</b> the outbox pattern: write the state and the event in one database transaction; a separate job publishes from the outbox. <code>lessons/13_events/README.md:104-106</code>" },
    ],
    qa: [
      { q: "Why run agents behind a queue and not inside the HTTP request?", a: "Runs are long and fail in the middle; requests must be short. A queue lets the API answer at once, lets stateless workers scale and restart, keeps work when a worker dies, and gives one place for limits. Trade-off: delivery is at-least-once, so consumers must be idempotent. <code>lab/bus.py:29-58</code>" },
      { q: "What is backpressure and how do you apply it?", a: "A queue moves the wait, it adds no capacity. Backpressure tells the caller the system is full: bound the backlog and answer 429 with Retry-After. In the lab the wait p95 went from 2.8 s (and growing) to 0.4 s, with half the calls refused. Trade-off: you refuse work. <code>lessons/13_events/run.py:47-74</code>" },
      { q: "What limits the throughput of an agent platform?", a: "Workers mostly wait on I/O, so the limit is usually the provider's rate limit, not your CPU. 16 workers behind a limit of 4 ran as fast as 4. Scale on queue depth and age, cap by quota, share the quota fairly over tenants. <code>lessons/13_events/run.py:30-44</code>" },
      { q: "What are event sourcing and CQRS, and why use them for agents?", a: "A run changes many times and you must explain it later. Event sourcing stores each change; state is computed from the events. CQRS: writes append events, reads use views built from them. You get audit, replay and cheap new views. Trade-off: read models lag, and long streams need snapshots. <code>lessons/13_events/run.py:117-161</code>" },
      { q: "What does at-least-once delivery mean for agent workers?", a: "The broker can't know if a worker that went silent did the work. So it gives the job again. The consumer must be idempotent: remember the ids it has done, or pass an idempotency key to the side effect. In the lab a plain consumer made 60 side effects for 50 jobs. <code>lab/bus.py:43-58</code> <code>lessons/13_events/run.py:84-91</code>" },
      { q: "What is a dead-letter queue for?", a: "A job that always fails would come back forever and block or burn tokens. So after N attempts it is moved aside. You alert on it, look at it, fix the cause, and replay it. Trade-off: someone must own that queue. <code>lab/bus.py:45-48</code>" },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "14", title: "Observability",
    job: "One trace per run: spans for LLM and tool calls.", entry: "lab/trace.py:44", uses: ["06"],
    cards: [
      { fp: true, t: "An agent decides its own path", b: "A normal service does the same thing for the same request. An agent chooses at run time how many steps to take and which tools to call. Averages hide that.<br><b>So:</b> record what each run did: one trace per run, one span per LLM call and per tool call, with tokens as data." },
      { t: "What goes into a trace?", b: "<b>Root span:</b> the run (tenant, task).<br><b>Child spans:</b> each LLM call (model, tokens in, out, cached) and each tool call (name, error).<br>Names follow the OpenTelemetry GenAI conventions, so any backend can read them. <code>lab/trace.py:23-41</code> <code>lab/trace.py:44-51</code>" },
      { t: "Where does the time go?", b: "<b>Evidence:</b> LLM calls took 87% of the run time; doc search 3%.<br><b>So:</b> making retrieval faster would change nothing. Read the traces before you optimize. <code>lessons/14_observability/run.py:60-68</code>" },
      { t: "Why p95, not the average?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> users meet the slow runs, and one slow span makes a slow run.<br><b>Evidence:</b> LLM call p50 1.3 s, p95 9.3 s. One market API call: 149 ms normally, 5.1 s once.<br><b>So:</b> give each step a timeout from the latency budget. <code>lessons/14_observability/run.py:21</code>" },
      { t: "Why decide sampling at the end?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> you can't store every trace, and head sampling decides before it knows what happened.<br><b>Evidence:</b> 25% head sampling kept 0 of the 2 interesting traces. Tail sampling (errors or over budget) kept both.<br><b>Cost:</b> something must hold the whole trace until the run ends. <code>lessons/14_observability/run.py:86-91</code>" },
      { t: "Threads lose the trace", b: "<b>Problem:</b> the loop runs tools in a thread pool. A new thread does not have the current span, so tool spans would become separate traces.<br><b>So:</b> copy the context into each thread. The same holds across queues and services: the trace id must travel in the message. <code>lab/loop.py:33-38</code>" },
      { t: "Four signals, four questions", b: "<b>Trace:</b> what did this run do?<br><b>Metric:</b> how is the system doing over time?<br><b>Log:</b> what were the details of one event?<br><b>Eval score:</b> was the answer good?<br>Latency and error rate alone can't tell you that an agent is wrong. <code>lessons/14_observability/README.md:24-29</code>" },
      { t: "A latency budget is split and passed down", b: "<b>Problem:</b> steps do not know how much time the run has left.<br><b>So:</b> start from what the user accepts (15 s), give each step a share and a timeout, and pass the deadline down. Near the end, degrade on purpose: fewer steps, faster model, shorter answer.<br><b>Evidence:</b> 1 of 8 runs was over budget; its longest span was one 9.3 s LLM call. <code>lessons/14_observability/README.md:113-125</code> <code>lessons/14_observability/run.py:70-83</code>" },
      { t: "Cost is a sum over spans", b: "<b>Problem:</b> the bill is one number. You need it per run, per tenant, per feature.<br><b>So:</b> put tokens (in, out, cached) on every LLM span, and tags on the root span. Then sum by any tag.<br><b>Evidence:</b> acme 17.6k tokens, beta 20.7k. <code>lab/trace.py:23-32</code> <code>lessons/14_observability/run.py:40-42</code>" },
    ],
    qa: [
      { q: "Why is observability harder for agents than for normal services?", a: "The path is chosen at run time: steps, tools and tokens differ per run, and the same input can take another path. So you need a trace per run with tokens and cost as data, plus quality scores, not only latency and error rate. Trade-off: traces hold prompts, so they need redaction and sampling. <code>lab/trace.py:44-51</code>" },
      { q: "Your agent is too slow. What do you do first?", a: "Guessing wastes time, so read the traces: which span type takes the time at p95? In the lab the LLM was 87% and one outside API had a 5 s tail. So: shorter outputs, thinking off for simple steps, a timeout on the tool. Faster retrieval (3%) would do nothing. <code>lessons/14_observability/run.py:60-68</code>" },
      { q: "Head sampling or tail sampling?", a: "Head sampling decides at the start, before it knows if the run fails; it is cheap and blind. Tail sampling decides at the end: keep errors and slow runs plus a small random share. Trade-off: a collector must buffer whole traces. <code>lessons/14_observability/run.py:86-91</code>" },
      { q: "How do you keep one trace across threads, queues and agents?", a: "The current span lives in the context of one thread of one process. Anything that leaves it must carry the context: copy it into worker threads, put the traceparent into queue messages, HTTP headers and worker-agent inputs. Otherwise each part shows up as its own small trace. <code>lab/loop.py:33-38</code>" },
      { q: "What do you put in a trace for an agent?", a: "You must be able to rebuild what the run did. So: a root span per run (tenant, session, task type), a child span per LLM call (model, input, output and cached tokens), a span per tool call (name, error), and spans for worker agents. Use the OpenTelemetry GenAI names so any backend can read them. <code>lab/trace.py:23-51</code>" },
      { q: "How do you track cost?", a: "Cost comes from tokens, and tokens are spent per LLM call. So record tokens on every LLM span with tenant and feature tags and sum by them. Watch cached tokens: they are cheaper, and a change that breaks the cache shows up there first. <code>lab/trace.py:27-31</code> <code>lessons/14_observability/run.py:40-42</code>" },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "15", title: "MCP",
    job: "Tools as servers, behind a gateway with roles.", entry: "lab/mcp_gateway.py:24", uses: ["06", "07"],
    cards: [
      { fp: true, t: "Tools inside the agent do not scale to many teams", b: "A tool as a function in the agent process means the agent needs that team's code, keys and deploys. With many teams and many agents that is N x M glue.<br><b>So:</b> a tool lives in a server with one protocol (list tools, call a tool). Then you need what any fleet needs: a registry, a gateway, permissions, audit." },
      { t: "What does the gateway do?", b: "It connects to every server in the registry, lists their tools, and gives the agent one catalog. Names get the server as a prefix, so two servers can both have a <code>search</code>.<br>Every call passes through it: one place for roles, limits and the audit log. <code>lab/mcp_gateway.py:29-42</code> <code>lab/mcp_gateway.py:58-65</code>" },
      { t: "Every tool is paid on every call", b: "<b>Problem:</b> tool schemas are part of the prompt of each LLM call.<br><b>Evidence:</b> 3 tools cost ~470 tokens per call, 28 tools ~3,431. The run cost 68% more, and the agent skipped the docs search.<br><b>So:</b> pick the few tools the task needs (by role, tag or search). <code>lab/mcp_gateway.py:52-56</code> <code>lessons/15_mcp/run.py:31-45</code>" },
      { t: "Hiding a tool is not a permission", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a hijacked or buggy agent can name a tool that is not in its list.<br><b>So:</b> check the role on every call, in code.<br><b>Evidence:</b> the reader role called <code>place_order</code> by name: 'DENIED by policy', and the refusal is in the audit log. <code>lab/mcp_gateway.py:47-50</code> <code>lab/mcp_gateway.py:58-62</code>" },
      { t: "Tool descriptions are untrusted text", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a description goes straight into your prompt, and the server can change it after you approved it.<br><b>So:</b> hash the tool definitions at approval; block the server when the hash changes.<br><b>Evidence:</b> a poisoned <code>get_price</code> description reached the prompt without the check; with it the server was blocked. <code>lab/mcp_gateway.py:18-21</code> <code>lab/mcp_gateway.py:36-39</code>" },
      { t: "The protocol is not the slow part", b: "<b>Evidence:</b> <code>search_docs</code> direct: 69 ms. Through the gateway and MCP: 71 ms. About 2 ms, next to LLM calls of 1-9 s.<br><b>Real cost:</b> one more process to run and to secure. <code>lessons/15_mcp/run.py:47-60</code>" },
      { t: "stdio or HTTP?", b: "<b>stdio:</b> the client starts the server as a child process and talks over stdin/stdout. No port, one client. Used in this lab.<br><b>HTTP:</b> a network service: many clients, auth, load balancing. A fleet needs this.<br>The gateway code is the same for both. <code>lessons/15_mcp/servers.py:52</code> <code>lab/mcp_gateway.py:31-33</code>" },
      { t: "Whose rights does the tool use?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a server with one powerful service account does whatever any agent asks. The user gets rights they do not have (confused deputy).<br><b>So:</b> the gateway passes the end user's scoped token. The tool acts as the user. <code>lessons/15_mcp/README.md:98-101</code>" },
      { t: "A tool schema is an API contract", b: "<b>Problem:</b> change a tool's name or description and no error appears. The agent just starts choosing badly.<br><b>So:</b> version tools, and run the evals on every tool change. <code>lessons/15_mcp/README.md:111-113</code>" },
      { t: "Why return errors as text?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a tool that raises gives the model 'Error executing tool' and nothing to act on.<br><b>So:</b> return the error as text ('ERROR: HTTP 400 ...'). The model can read it and recover. <code>lessons/15_mcp/servers.py:12-17</code> <code>lab/tools.py:61-68</code>" },
    ],
    qa: [
      { q: "What is MCP and what problem does it solve?", a: "Tools built into each agent mean custom glue for every agent-tool pair. MCP is one protocol between agents and tool servers: list tools, call tools. A tool is written once, owned by one team, used by any agent. Trade-off: each server is a dependency whose text enters your prompt. <code>lessons/15_mcp/servers.py:12-34</code>" },
      { q: "The agent has no place_order in its tool list. Is that enough?", a: "No. The list only guides the model; it is not enforcement. The call path must check the permission again with the caller's identity, on every call. In the lab the gateway denied the reader role when it named the hidden tool. <code>lab/mcp_gateway.py:58-62</code> <code>lessons/15_mcp/run.py:62-75</code>" },
      { q: "What breaks when an agent can see 200 tools?", a: "Schemas are sent on every call, so cost grows with the list (470 vs 3,431 tokens here). The model also chooses worse among many similar tools, and any change in the list breaks the prompt cache. So select tools per task and keep the list stable in a session. <code>lab/mcp_gateway.py:52-56</code>" },
      { q: "What are the security risks of MCP?", a: "Descriptions and results are untrusted text in your prompt (tool poisoning). A server can change its tools after approval (rug pull). A server can act with more rights than the user (confused deputy). Defences: review and pin definitions, user-scoped tokens, deny by default at a gateway, approval for risky tools, audit. <code>lab/mcp_gateway.py:18-21</code> <code>lessons/15_mcp/run.py:77-86</code>" },
      { q: "Why put a gateway in front of MCP servers?", a: "With many servers, every one would need auth, permissions, rate limits and audit, and every agent would need every address. A gateway is one place for all of that and one entry point. Servers stay simple. Trade-off: a shared hop that must be replicated. <code>lab/mcp_gateway.py:24-65</code>" },
      { q: "stdio or HTTP transport?", a: "stdio: the client starts the server as a child process. Simple, local, no port, one client. HTTP: a network service with many clients, auth and scaling. Use stdio for local tools and tests, HTTP for a fleet. <code>lessons/15_mcp/servers.py:52</code>" },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "16", title: "Frameworks",
    job: "One interface, two runtimes: own loop and LangGraph.", entry: "lab/runtime.py:23", uses: ["06"],
    cards: [
      { fp: true, t: "Frameworks change faster than your product", b: "No agent framework has won, and they change often. If product code calls a framework everywhere, a framework change is a rewrite.<br><b>So:</b> product code calls your own small interface (<code>run() -> Result</code>). A framework is one adapter behind it." },
      { t: "What is the interface?", b: "<code>run(question)</code> returns a <code>Result</code>: answer, steps, tokens, trace. Two adapters: the lesson 6 loop and LangGraph. Both use the same model client and the same tools, so a difference in numbers comes from the runtime. <code>lab/runtime.py:16-33</code>" },
      { t: "The framework did not change the answers", b: "<b>Evidence:</b> own loop 10/10 at 2.1 steps. LangGraph 10/10 at 2.1 steps.<br><b>Why:</b> same model, same tools, same prompts.<br><b>So:</b> choose a framework for what it gives around the model, not for quality. <code>lessons/16_framework/run.py:45-59</code>" },
      { t: "What LangGraph adds", b: "An explicit state machine: typed state, nodes that return updates, edges decided in code, a checkpoint after every node.<br><b>Evidence:</b> the run paused before <code>place_order</code>, the state was saved, and a later call resumed it.<br><b>Cost:</b> new dependencies and their release cycle. <code>lab/runtime.py:43-56</code> <code>lab/runtime.py:68-72</code>" },
      { t: "How do you know a port is right?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> 'it answered my question' proves nothing.<br><b>So:</b> contract tests through the interface (step limit, tool error, trace), then the same evals on both sides.<br><b>Evidence:</b> 4 of 4 contract tests pass on both runtimes. <code>lessons/16_framework/run.py:27-35</code>" },
      { t: "Break it before you trust it", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a demo shows only the easy path.<br><b>So:</b> port one real agent, run the same evals, then break it: kill the process in the middle, fail a tool, hit the step limit, run 100 at once. Check that you can see the exact messages sent to the model. <code>lessons/16_framework/README.md:98-112</code>" },
      { t: "Migrate in steps, with a shadow run", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a switch in one day puts every run at risk at once.<br><b>So:</b> interface first, then contract tests and evals, then the new adapter. Shadow run: both get real traffic, the old answer is served. Move traffic in steps. Old runs finish on the old runtime.<br><b>Limit:</b> shadow only read-only tasks; side effects must not run twice. <code>lessons/16_framework/README.md:114-124</code>" },
      { t: "What a framework costs", b: "New dependencies with their own release cycle. Debugging through its layers. A state format you must keep readable across versions. And prebuilt agents can hide the prompt and the retries.<br><b>Here:</b> nodes are plain functions, so every message stays visible. <code>lessons/16_framework/README.md:76-86</code> <code>lab/runtime.py:58-66</code>" },
    ],
    qa: [
      { q: "Framework or your own loop?", a: "The loop is about 40 lines; it is not the hard part. The hard parts are state, resume, approval, streaming, tracing. Use a framework when it gives those and still lets you see every message. Either way keep product code behind your own interface. Trade-off: an adapter to maintain. <code>lab/loop.py:18-43</code> <code>lab/runtime.py:23-33</code>" },
      { q: "Why an abstraction layer over agent frameworks?", a: "Frameworks change faster than products. A thin interface makes a framework one adapter and gives one place for tracing, budgets and policy. Keep it thin: if it grows into a framework of its own, you have the same problem again. <code>lab/runtime.py:16-24</code>" },
      { q: "How would you evaluate a new framework?", a: "A demo shows the easy path. So port one real agent behind the interface, run the same evals, then break it: kill the process, fail a tool, hit the step limit, run many at once. Check that you can see the exact messages and plug in your gateway and tracing. <code>lessons/16_framework/run.py:27-35</code>" },
      { q: "How do you migrate from one runtime to another?", a: "Runs in flight and hidden behaviour make a big-bang switch risky. Put the interface in front of the old runtime, add contract tests and evals, build the new adapter until they pass, shadow-run read-only traffic, move traffic in steps behind a flag, and let old runs finish on the old runtime. <code>lab/runtime.py:27-33</code>" },
      { q: "What does LangGraph add over a while loop?", a: "A while loop keeps its state in local variables, which die with the process. LangGraph makes the state explicit: typed state, nodes, edges, and a checkpoint after every node. From that come resume, pause for a human, and state history. It scored the same as the loop (10/10): the gain is operations, not quality. <code>lab/runtime.py:36-56</code>" },
      { q: "What are the risks of depending on a framework?", a: "You depend on code you do not control: breaking changes, hidden prompts and retries, hidden token cost, hard debugging through layers, and lock-in of your state format. A thin interface of your own limits the damage. <code>lessons/16_framework/README.md:76-86</code>" },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "17", title: "Runtime as a service",
    job: "HTTP API in front of the queue: 202, idempotency, 429.", entry: "lab/service.py:28", uses: ["13", "06"],
    cards: [
      { fp: true, t: "The API must never wait for a run", b: "A run is slow and can fail; many clients call at once and they retry. If the API waits, requests time out and retries start runs twice.<br><b>So:</b> POST returns 202 and a run id at once. The run goes to the queue. Status and events are read separately." },
      { t: "Why 202 and a run id?", b: "<b>Evidence:</b> 100 runs sent at once: POST p95 93 ms, while the runs finished over 2.4 s.<br>The client gets an id at once and reads progress by polling or from the event stream. <code>lab/service.py:56-73</code> <code>lab/service.py:84-95</code>" },
      { t: "Why an idempotency key?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a POST times out. The client does not know if the run started, so it sends it again.<br><b>So:</b> the server maps (tenant, key) to the first run and returns it.<br><b>Evidence:</b> 5 retries: 5 runs without the key, 1 with. <code>lab/service.py:57-60</code> <code>lessons/17_service/run.py:87-92</code>" },
      { t: "Why refuse work?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> under overload every accepted run waits longer.<br><b>Evidence:</b> 300 at once: p95 7.1 s with no limit. With at most 40 unfinished runs: p95 1.0 s, 260 refused with 429.<br><b>So:</b> set the limit from the latency you promise. <code>lab/service.py:61-64</code> <code>lessons/17_service/run.py:81-85</code>" },
      { t: "One tenant can take the whole runtime", b: "<b>Problem:</b> one shared FIFO queue serves whoever sent first.<br><b>Evidence:</b> 5 runs of a small tenant behind 120 of a big one: 3.1 s. With a per-tenant limit: 0.56 s.<br><b>Cost:</b> the big tenant is refused, not just slowed. Fair queues would be better. <code>lab/service.py:63-64</code> <code>lessons/17_service/run.py:94-103</code>" },
      { t: "Workers hold no state", b: "A worker takes a job, runs the agent, writes events, acks. All state is in the queue and the event store, so any worker can take any job, and you can add or restart workers freely.<br><b>Limit of this lab:</b> the stores are in memory; a restart loses runs. Lesson 9 fixes that. <code>lab/service.py:97-115</code>" },
      { t: "Progress as events", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a run takes seconds to minutes. A client that sees nothing thinks it is stuck.<br><b>So:</b> the worker writes events (started, tool called, finished); the client reads them as a stream.<br><b>Evidence:</b> 11 s passed between the tool call and the end: one LLM call. For chat, stream the model's tokens as events too. <code>lab/service.py:84-95</code> <code>lessons/17_service/run.py:24-37</code>" },
      { t: "Never trust the tenant in the body", b: "<b>Problem:</b> this lab reads <code>tenant</code> from the request body. Any caller can write any name there.<br><b>So:</b> in production the tenant comes from the verified token, set by the gateway in front. Limits and data scope follow from it. <code>lab/service.py:23-25</code> <code>lessons/17_service/README.md:92-102</code>" },
      { t: "Load test with a fake model first", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a load test with a real model is slow, costs money, and mixes provider noise into your numbers.<br><b>So:</b> first a fake agent (a 0.2 s sleep): it shows API latency, queue wait, overload and fairness. Then a smaller test with the real model for provider limits and cost. <code>lessons/17_service/run.py:17-21</code>" },
    ],
    qa: [
      { q: "Design the API for a long-running agent.", a: "A run is slow and the request must be fast. So POST returns 202 and a run id; the work goes to a queue. GET for status, an event stream for progress. Idempotency key on POST because clients retry. 429 with Retry-After when full. Trade-off: the client must handle an async flow. <code>lab/service.py:35-40</code> <code>lab/service.py:56-73</code>" },
      { q: "Why an idempotency key when the queue already dedupes?", a: "The retry happens before the queue: the client's POST timed out and it can't know if the run exists. Only the server can map (tenant, key) to the first run and return it. The lab made 5 runs from 5 retries without the key and 1 with it. <code>lab/service.py:57-60</code>" },
      { q: "How do you stop one tenant from starving the others?", a: "A shared FIFO queue serves the first sender. So limit each tenant at admission and schedule fairly (a queue per tenant, taken in turn). In the lab a small tenant waited 3.1 s behind a big one, and 0.56 s with a limit. Tenant identity must come from the token, not the request body. <code>lab/service.py:63-64</code>" },
      { q: "How do you scale this to hundreds of agents?", a: "Scaling needs workers that can be added and lost freely, so they must be stateless: state lives in the queue, the workflow engine and the database. Autoscale on queue depth and wait, capped by provider quota; partition by tenant; one LLM gateway; trace everything. <code>lab/service.py:97-115</code> <code>docs/design/agent-runtime.md:24-40</code>" },
      { q: "How do you choose the queue limit?", a: "A queue only moves the wait, so the limit decides how long accepted work waits. Start from the latency you promise: limit = target wait x service rate. Then measure. In the lab, no limit gave p95 7 s for 300 runs; a limit of 40 kept it at 1 s and the rest got a fast 429. <code>lab/service.py:61-62</code> <code>lessons/17_service/run.py:81-85</code>" },
      { q: "What do you load test in an agent runtime?", a: "Two things fail under load: the runtime and the provider. Test them apart. With a fake model: API latency, queue wait, overload, fairness, a restart in the middle. That finds runtime bugs cheaply. Then a smaller test with the real model for rate limits and cost. <code>lessons/17_service/run.py:17-21</code> <code>lessons/17_service/run.py:76-103</code>" },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "18", title: "Design: runtime at scale",
    job: "The whole runtime on AWS / EKS: decisions and trade-offs (design only).", entry: "docs/design/agent-runtime.md:19", uses: ["09", "10", "11", "13", "14", "15", "16", "17"],
    cards: [
      { fp: true, t: "Every agent team hits the same hard problems", b: "A run that survives a crash. A provider that fails. A tool that needs a permission. A cost nobody can explain. If each team solves these alone, you get N different answers.<br><b>So:</b> a shared runtime solves them once. Agent teams bring only the agent.<br>This lesson is a design document. Nothing in it was run in this repo. <code>docs/design/agent-runtime.md:19-45</code>" },
      { t: "Why a workflow engine, not checkpoints in a database?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> runs are long, wait for humans, and have side effects. With hand-made checkpoints every team builds retries, timers and versioning again.<br><b>So:</b> Temporal.<br><b>Cost:</b> deterministic workflow code, payload limits, one more system to run. <code>docs/design/agent-runtime.md:49-54</code>" },
      { t: "Why is the agent loop the workflow?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> one big activity per run can't resume inside the run.<br><b>So:</b> the loop is the workflow; each LLM call and tool call is an activity. Replay keeps the one answer the model gave.<br><b>Cost:</b> history grows with every step; very long runs need continue-as-new. <code>docs/design/agent-runtime.md:56-59</code>" },
      { t: "Why are the gateways shared services?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> keys, quotas, failover and permissions in every agent means N copies and N leaks.<br><b>So:</b> one LLM gateway and one MCP gateway.<br><b>Cost:</b> a shared failure point. So they are stateless and replicated, and tools fail closed. <code>docs/design/agent-runtime.md:66-70</code>" },
      { t: "Why isolate tenants by partition, not by cluster?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a cluster per tenant is safe but costly, and slow to roll out.<br><b>So:</b> shared compute; every store keyed by tenant; quotas at admission; fair queues. The tenant id comes from the token.<br><b>Cost:</b> a noisy tenant is limited, not removed. A tenant with strict rules can get its own namespace. <code>docs/design/agent-runtime.md:76-80</code>" },
      { t: "Each kind of state has its own store", b: "<b>Run history:</b> Temporal. <b>Status and idempotency keys:</b> DynamoDB, with a time to live. <b>Cache and rate counters:</b> ElastiCache (Redis). <b>Memory:</b> Postgres with pgvector. <b>Big payloads:</b> S3; the history keeps only the key.<br><b>Why split:</b> they differ in lifetime, size and access pattern. <code>docs/design/agent-runtime.md:101-110</code>" },
      { t: "Why scale on backlog, not CPU?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> an agent worker waits on I/O. Its CPU is low even when it is full.<br><b>So:</b> autoscale on queue backlog and wait time (KEDA on EKS), and cap the replicas by the provider quota.<br><b>Evidence (lesson 13):</b> 16 workers behind a limit of 4 were as fast as 4. <code>docs/design/agent-runtime.md:112-121</code> <code>lessons/13_events/run.py:30-44</code>" },
      { t: "Why not Lambda for agent runs?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a run can take longer than Lambda's 15 minutes, and cold starts hurt chat latency.<br><b>So:</b> runs on EKS workers. Lambda for small event handlers (webhooks, memory writes). Bedrock for managed models, behind the LLM gateway; SageMaker only for models we host. <code>docs/design/agent-runtime.md:122-127</code>" },
      { t: "Why separate pools for chat and batch?", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a big batch job fills the workers and chat users wait.<br><b>So:</b> separate task queues and worker pools. Risky tools (code execution) get a sandboxed pool with no network. <code>docs/design/agent-runtime.md:119-121</code>" },
      { t: "Prompts are releases", b: "<b>Problem:</b> a prompt, a tool schema or a model version changes behaviour like code does, but often ships with no test.<br><b>So:</b> one pipeline for all: tests, contract tests, eval gate, image, then a canary rollout with automatic rollback. Infrastructure is Terraform; Git is the desired state (ArgoCD). <code>docs/design/agent-runtime.md:142-154</code>" },
      { t: "Name each failure and its answer", b: "Worker dies: replay. Provider down: fallback and breaker. Rate limit: bounded queue, 429. Poison run: dead letter. Agent loops: one budget for the tree. Injection: permissions in code. Bad deploy: canary and eval gate.<br>A design is ready when every row has an answer. <code>docs/design/agent-runtime.md:156-168</code>" },
      { t: "What to promise and watch", b: "Run success rate. Time to first token and full answer at p95. Queue wait at p95. Cost per run and per tenant. Quality: eval score on sampled traces.<br><b>Why these:</b> each one is what a user or the budget feels, and each one points to a part of the system. <code>docs/design/agent-runtime.md:170-176</code>" },
    ],
    qa: [
      { q: "Design the platform that runs our agents.", a: "Root: runs are long, costly and fail in the middle, and many tenants share the system. So: an API that answers 202; a workflow engine where the loop is the workflow and each LLM or tool call is an activity; stateless workers; shared LLM and MCP gateways; state in stores keyed by tenant; autoscale on backlog; one trace per run. Trade-off: several systems to run. <code>docs/design/agent-runtime.md:19-45</code>" },
      { q: "Temporal, Step Functions, or your own checkpoints?", a: "The need is durable steps, timers, human waits and safe code changes. Own checkpoints: fine for one service, but each team builds the rest again. Step Functions: managed, good on AWS, weaker for long dynamic loops and local tests. Temporal: fits loops and waits; cost is deterministic code and running it (or paying for the cloud version). <code>docs/design/agent-runtime.md:49-54</code>" },
      { q: "How do you scale workers on Kubernetes?", a: "Workers wait on I/O, so CPU says nothing. Scale on queue backlog and wait time (KEDA), cap replicas by the provider quota, keep pods stateless so a rolling deploy loses no run, and use separate pools for chat and batch. <code>docs/design/agent-runtime.md:112-121</code>" },
      { q: "How do you ship a prompt change safely?", a: "A prompt changes behaviour like code, so it gets the same pipeline: version it, run the eval gate against the baseline, roll out as a canary, watch the SLOs, roll back on a drop. Runs in flight finish on the old version. <code>docs/design/agent-runtime.md:142-154</code>" },
      { q: "Where does each kind of state live, and why not one database?", a: "They differ in lifetime, size and access. Run history in the engine (the truth). Status and idempotency keys in a key-value store with a time to live. Counters and cache in Redis. Memory in a database with a vector index. Big payloads in a blob store. One database for all would be slow for some and costly for others. <code>docs/design/agent-runtime.md:101-110</code>" },
    ],
  },
];
